| 4 | **No HTTPS enforcement** | All traffic is plain HTTP, which allows eavesdropping and man‑in‑the‑middle attacks. | Deploy behind a TLS‑terminating proxy, or add `app.use((req,res,next)=>{ if(req.secure) return next(); res.redirect(`https://${req.headers.host}${req.url}`);});` for forced HTTPS (only when you control the TLS termination). |
| 5 | **No input validation / sanitisation** | The current routes don’t accept any user input, but adding new endpoints without validation can introduce injection attacks (SQL/NoSQL, command injection, XSS, etc.). | When you start accepting parameters, use a validation library such as **Joi**, **express-validator**, or **zod**, and always whitelist/escape data before using it. |
| 6 | **No error‑handling middleware** | Uncaught errors will result in default Express error responses that may leak stack traces or internal details. | Add a generic error handler at the end of the middleware chain: <br>`app.use((err, req, res, next) => { console.error(err); res.status(500).json({error: 'Internal Server Error'}); });` |
| 7 | **No CORS configuration** | If the API is consumed by browsers, unrestricted CORS (`*`) can be risky. Conversely, no CORS header may break legitimate front‑ends. | Explicitly set allowed origins using the **cors** package: <br>`const cors = require('cors');`<br>`app.use(cors({origin: ['https://myapp.example.com']}));` |
| 8 | **Static user data exposed** | The `/users` endpoint returns hard‑coded personal data (names, emails). In a real system this would be private information. | Protect the endpoint with authentication/authorization (e.g., JWT, session) and only return data the caller is allowed to see. |
| 9 | **No dependency lockfile / version pinning** | The repo lacks a `package.json`/`package-lock.json` (or `yarn.lock`) so we can’t audit third‑party packages for known vulnerabilities (e.g., outdated Express versions). | Add a proper `package.json` with exact versions, run `npm audit`/`yarn audit`, and keep dependencies up to date. |
|10| **Potential environment‑variable leakage** | While not present now, exporting the Express app (`module.exports = app;`) could be used by other modules that inadvertently expose `process.env`. | When adding more modules, avoid passing raw `process.env` objects to templates or logs; use a whitelist. |

